he character expansion gives:\n```\nK(U⁺, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺))\n```\n\nFor this to be PD, we need `F(w) · Ψ_w(u⁰) ≥ 0` for all w. But `Ψ_w(u⁰)` is a product of characters, which is generally complex. So this is NOT automatically PD for each u⁰.\n\nHmm, so there's a real issue here. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the structure of the interface action. The interface plaquettes involve links from U⁺, u⁰, and V⁺ (= θW⁺). But which links exactly?\n\nIn the Osterwalder-Seiler setup, the interface plaquettes are those that cross the t=0 plane. Each such plaquette has:\n- Some links at t=0 (interface, u⁰)\n- Some links at t=1 (positive, U⁺) or t=-1 (negative, V⁺)\n\nThe key point is that the interface links u⁰ appear in the kernel. For the kernel to be PD for each fixed u⁰, we need the character expansion to give non-negative coefficients for each u⁰.\n\nBut actually, I think the correct argument is different. Let me reconsider.\n\nIn the Osterwalder-Seiler proof, the reflection positivity is proved by showing that the Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S)` is reflection-positive. This means:\n```\n∫ F(U⁺) · conj(F(θU⁺)) · exp(-β S(U)) dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nfor all F depending on the positive half. The proof uses the fact that each plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD, and the product of PD factors (over the interface plaquettes) gives a PD kernel.\n\nThe key insight is that the interface plaquettes can be grouped so that the kernel factors as a product of PD kernels on shared variables. Specifically, each interface plaquette involves 4 links, some from U⁺, some from u⁰, some from V⁺. The PD of each plaquette Boltzmann factor (on its 4 link variables) means that the product over all interface plaquettes is PD on the combined set of link variables.\n\nBut \"PD on the combined set\" means:\n```\n∫∫ F(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(F(W⁺, u⁰')) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺, u⁰') dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nwhere `K` is the product of plaquette Boltzmann factors. This is PD on the space (U⁺, u⁰) × (W⁺, u⁰'). But in our integral, u⁰ = u⁰' (the same interface config appears on both sides). So we need:\n```\n∫∫ F(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(F(W⁺, u⁰)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁺(W⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThis is NOT the same as \"PD for each fixed u⁰\". It's the integral of a PD kernel evaluated at u⁰ = u⁰'.\n\nHmm, actually, I think the correct statement is:\n\nThe kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺, u⁰') = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` (where S_int doesn't depend on u⁰' separately — it only depends on u⁰) is PD on (U⁺, u⁰) × (W⁺, u⁰'). Wait, but S_int depends on u⁰, not u⁰'. So the kernel is `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))`, which doesn't depend on u⁰' at all. That means:\n```\n∫∫ F(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(F(W⁺, u⁰')) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁺(W⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰) dμ⁰(u⁰')\n= ∫ [∫ F(U⁺, u⁰) dμ⁰(u⁰)] · conj(∫ F(W⁺, u⁰') dμ⁰(u⁰')) · ... \n```\n\nNo, that's not right either. Let me think more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the interface action `S_int` couples U⁺, u⁰, and V⁺ (= θW⁺) together. The kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` is a function of THREE variables: U⁺, u⁰, and W⁺. \n\nIn our integral, we have:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(H(W⁺, u⁰_s)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁺(W⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nNote that `H` depends on u⁰_s (spatial interface links), and `K` depends on the full u⁰ (both spatial and temporal interface links).\n\nThe PD argument: the kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺)` is PD as a kernel on the space (U⁺, u⁰) × (W⁺, u⁰). But in our integral, the SAME u⁰ appears on both sides (both in H and in K). So we're evaluating:\n```\n∫_{u⁰} ∫∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(H(W⁺, u⁰_s)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺ dμ⁺ dμ⁰\n```\n\nIf we define `F(U⁺, u⁰) = H(U⁺, u⁰_s)` (which depends on u⁰ only through its spatial part), then:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫∫ F(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(F(W⁺, u⁰)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺ dμ⁺ dμ⁰\n```\n\nAnd if `K` is PD as a kernel on (U⁺, u⁰) × (W⁺, u⁰), then:\n```\n∫∫∫ F(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(F(W⁺, u⁰)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁺(W⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰) ≥