d u⁰.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The product of PD functions is PD. So `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = ∏_p B_p(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺)` is PD on the product space (U⁺ × u⁰ × W⁺). But \"PD on the product space\" means:\n```\n∫ F(x) · conj(F(y)) · K(x, y) dμ(x) dμ(y) ≥ 0\n```\n\nwhere `x = (U⁺, u⁰, W⁺)` and `y = (U⁺', u⁰', W⁺')`. But `K(x, y) = K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺)` doesn't depend on `y` at all! That can't be right.\n\nI think I'm confusing the notion of PD. Let me reconsider.\n\nA function `K: G → ℂ` on a group `G` is PD if `∑_{i,j} c_i conj(c_j) K(g_i⁻¹ g_j) ≥ 0` for all `c_i, g_j`. Equivalently, `∫∫ f(g) conj(f(h)) K(g⁻¹ h) dμ(g) dμ(h) ≥ 0`.\n\nFor the plaquette Boltzmann factor `B(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃ g₄))`, this is PD on `SU(N)⁴` (as a group). The PD means:\n```\n∫∫ F(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) · conj(F(h₁, h₂, h₃, h₄)) · B(g₁⁻¹h₁, g₂⁻¹h₂, g₃⁻¹h₃, g₄⁻¹h₄) dμ dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nBut the interface kernel is a PRODUCT of such factors, where different plaquettes share link variables. The product is PD on the product group (all link variables), but the PD involves the group structure (i.e., `K(g⁻¹ h)` where `g` and `h` are full configurations).\n\nIn the reflection positivity setup, the kernel is NOT of the form `K(g⁻¹ h)`. Instead, it's `K(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺)` where `θ` is the reflection. The reflection positivity is a DIFFERENT kind of PD — it's PD with respect to the reflection, not the group inverse.\n\nOK, I think I need to understand the actual Osterwalder-Seiler argument better. Let me look at the character expansion approach.\n\nThe character expansion of the interface kernel gives:\n```\nK(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(W⁺))\n```\n\nwhere `Φ_w(U⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ L_U} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`, `Ψ_w(u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ L_0} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`, and the W⁺ factor uses the dual.\n\nThen:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(H(W⁺, u⁰_s)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺ dμ⁺ dμ⁰\n= ∑_w F(w) ∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · |∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺(U⁺)|² dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nWait, this uses the fact that `∫∫ H(U⁺) · 