eed to be integrated out, and the `|...|²` term has a specific structure that makes the integral non-negative.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that `H(U⁺, u⁰_s) = f(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` depends on u⁰_s. So `|∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺|²` is a function of u⁰_s. And we're integrating `∏_{l ∈ L_0_spatial} χ_{w(l)}(g_l) · |...(u⁰_s)|²` over u⁰_s.\n\nThis is `∫ χ(g) · |F(g)|² dμ(g)` where `χ` is a character and `F` is some function. This is NOT necessarily non-negative.\n\nSo I'm stuck. The character expansion approach does NOT directly give non-negativity, even after integrating over u⁰. This is consistent with the §8.11.58 finding that the matrix is NOT PSD.\n\nSo what IS the correct argument? Let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, maybe the correct argument is NOT the character expansion at all. Maybe it's the direct PD of the plaquette Boltzmann factors, combined in a specific way.\n\nThe Osterwalder-Seiler reflection positivity proof works as follows:\n1. The Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S)` is a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors.\n2. Each plaquette Boltzmann factor `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁g₂g₃g₄))` is PD on `SU(N)⁴`.\n3. The product of PD factors is PD (on the product group).\n4. The reflection positivity follows from the PD of the Boltzmann factor.\n\nBut step 3 requires the PD to be on the GROUP (i.e., `K(g⁻¹ h)`), not on the reflection. The reflection positivity is:\n```\n∫ F(U⁺) · conj(F(θU⁺)) · exp(-β S(U)) dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nThis is NOT the standard group PD. It's a reflection PD. The connection between group PD and reflection PD is non-trivial.\n\nActually, I think the correct argument is:\n\nThe reflection positivity integral can be written as:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) · conj(F(θU⁺)) · ∏_p B_p(U) dμ(U)\n```\n\nwhere `B_p` is the plaquette Boltzmann factor for plaquette `p`. The plaquettes can be divided into:\n- Positive plaquettes (entirely in the positive half): `B_p` depends only on U⁺.\n- Negative plaquettes (entirely in the negative half): `B_p`