{"id":67,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\PositiveDefiniteIntegral.lean\n-- 1544 lines, 80022 bytes --\n540 | continuous kernel `K` that is positive-definite in the Mercer sense, and a\n541 | continuous function `f`, the integral\n542 | `∫∫ f(x) * conj(f(y)) * K(x, y) dμ dμ ≥ 0`.\n543 | \n544 | This is the Mercer-type generalization of\n545 | `PositiveDefinite.integralOperator_nonneg`: it removes the group-structure\n546 | requirement, so the kernel `K(x, y)` can be any continuous function of two\n547 | variables (not just `φ(x⁻¹ * y)` for a PD function on a group).  The proof\n548 | is the same Riemann-sum argument: approximate the integral by Riemann sums\n549 | (each non-negative by `PositiveDefiniteKernel.sum_nonneg_of_map`) and control\n550 | the error via uniform continuity on the compact space `X × X`.\n551 | \n552 | No Haar-measure invariance or group structure is needed. -/\n553 | lemma PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg\n554 |     {X : Type*} [PseudoMetricSpace X] [CompactSpace X]\n555 |     [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X] [SecondCountableTopology X]\n556 |     (μ : Measure X) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]\n557 |     {K : X → X → ℂ} (hK : PositiveDefiniteKernel K)\n558 |     (hK_cont : Continuous (Function.uncurry K))\n559 |     {f : X → ℂ} (hf_cont : Continuous f) :\n560 |     0 ≤ ∫ x, ∫ y, f x * conj (f y) * K x y ∂μ ∂μ := by\n561 |   -- The integrand as a function on X × X\n562 |   let F : X × X → ℂ := fun p => f p.1 * conj (f p.2) * K p.1 p.2\n563 |   -- F is continuous (product of continuous functions; no group structure needed)\n564 |   have hF_cont : Continuous F := by\n565 |     have h_fst : Continuous (fun p : X × X => f p.1) := hf_cont.comp continuous_fst\n566 |     have h_snd : Continuous (fun p : X × X => f p.2) := hf_cont.comp continuous_snd\n567 |     have h_conj_snd : Continuous (fun p : X × X => conj (f p.2)) :=\n568 |       Complex.continuous_conj.comp h_snd\n569 |     have h_K : Continuous (fun p : X × X => K p.1 p.2) := hK_cont\n570 |     convert Continuous.mul (Continuous.mul h_fst h_conj_snd) h_K using 1\n571 |     ext p; rfl\n572 |   -- X × X is compact\n573 |   haveI : CompactSpace (X × X) := inferInstance\n574 |   -- F is uniformly continuous (Heine–Cantor)\n575 |   have hF_unif : UniformContinuous F :=\n576 |     CompactSpace.uniformContinuous_of_continuous hF_cont\n577 |   -- F is integrable w.r.t. μ.prod μ (continuous on compact → bounded → integrable)\n578 |   have hF_aes : AEStronglyMeasurable F (μ.prod μ) :=\n579 |     Continuous.aestronglyMeasurable_of_compactSpace hF_cont\n580 |   have hF_bdd : ∃ C : ℝ, 0 ≤ C ∧ ∀ z : X × X, ‖F z‖ ≤ C := by\n581 |     have h_range_compact : IsCompact (Set.range F) := by\n582 |       rw [← Set.image_univ]\n583 |       exact isCompact_univ.image hF_cont\n584 |     obtain ⟨C, hCpos, hC⟩ := h_range_compact.isBounded.exists_pos_norm_le\n585 |     exact ⟨C, hCpos.le, fun z => hC (F z) (Set.mem_range_self _)⟩\n586 |   obtain ⟨C, hCnn, hC⟩ := hF_bdd\n587 |   have hF_int : Integrable F (μ.prod μ) := by\n588 |     apply Integrable.of_bound hF_aes C\n589 |     filter_upwards with z using hC z\n590 |   -- Convert the iterated integral to a product-measure integral (Fubini)\n591 |   have hFub : ∫ z, F z ∂(μ.prod μ) = ∫ x, ∫ y, f x * conj (f y) * K x y ∂μ ∂μ := by\n592 |     rw [integral_prod F hF_int]\n593 |   rw [← hFub]\n594 |   -- Goal: 0 ≤ ∫ z, F z ∂(μ.prod μ)\n595 |   set I : ℂ := ∫ z, F z ∂(μ.prod μ)\n596 |   -- Strategy: for every ε > 0, build a Riemann sum S ≥ 0 with ‖I - S‖ ≤ ε.\n597 |   -- Then Re(I) ≥ 0 and Im(I) = 0, hence 0 ≤ I.\n598 |   have hkey : ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∃ S : ℂ, 0 ≤ S ∧ ‖I - S‖ ≤ ε := by\n599 |     intro ε εpos\n600 |     -- δ from uniform continuity of F on X × X\n601 |     obtain ⟨δ, δpos, hδ⟩ := Metric.uniformContinuous_iff.mp hF_unif ε εpos\n602 |     -- Cover X by finitely many δ/2-balls\n603 |     obtain ⟨t, _, ht_fin, ht_cover⟩ :=\n604 |       finite_cover_balls_of_compact (isCompact_univ : IsCompact (Set.univ : Set X)) (half_pos δpos)\n605 |     -- Enumerate the finite cover\n606 |     let ts : Finset X := ht_fin.toFinset\n607 |     have hts_eq : ↑ts = t := ht_fin.coe_toFinset\n608 |     -- Enumeration: Fin n ≃ ↥ts\n609 |     let n := ts.card\n610 |     let e : Fin n ≃ ↥ts := ts.equivFin.symm\n611 |     let x : Fin n → X := fun i => (e i : X)\n612 |     -- Balls B_i = ball (x_i, δ/2)\n613 |     let B : Fin n → Set X := fun i => ball (x i) (δ / 2)\n614 |     -- Properties of disjointed B\n615 |     have hA_disjoint : Pairwise (Disjoint on disjointed B) := disjoint_disjointed B\n616 |     have hA_cover : (Set.univ : Set X) ⊆ ⋃ (i : Fin n), disjointed B i := by\n617 |       rw [iUnion_disjointed]\n618 |       intro g hg\n619 |       simp only [Set.mem_iUnion]\n620 |       have hg' := ht_cover hg\n621 |       simp only [Set.mem_iUnion₂] at hg'\n622 |       obtain ⟨xg, hxg, hg_ball⟩ := hg'\n623 |       have hxg_ts : xg ∈ ts := by\n624 |         have : xg ∈ (↑ts : Set X) := hts_eq.symm ▸ hxg\n625 |         exact Finset.mem_coe.mp this\n626 |       obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ := (Equiv.surjective e) ⟨xg, hxg_ts⟩\n627 |       refine ⟨i, ?_⟩\n628 |       have hxi : x i = xg := by simp only [x, hi]\n629 |       simp only [B, hxi]\n630 |       exact hg_ball\n631 |     have hA_meas : ∀ (i : Fin n), MeasurableSet (disjointed B i) := by\n632 |       intro i\n633 |       rw [disjointed_apply, Finset.sup_set_eq_biUnion]\n634 |       exact MeasurableSet.diff isOpen_ball.measurableSet\n635 |         (Finset.measurableSet_biUnion _ (fun j _ => isOpen_ball.measurableSet))\n636 |     -- The Riemann sum: S = ∑_{i,j} c_i * conj(c_j) * K(x_i, x_j) ≥ 0\n637 |     let c : Fin n → ℂ := fun i => f (x i) * ((μ.real (disjointed B i)) : ℂ)\n638 |     refine ⟨∑ i, ∑ j, c i * conj (c j) * K (x i) (x j), ?_, ?_⟩\n639 |     · -- S ≥ 0 by PositiveDefiniteKernel.sum_nonneg_of_map\n640 |       exact hK.sum_nonneg_of_map Finset.univ x c\n641 |     · -- ‖I - S‖ ≤ ε\n642 |       -- Partition X × X into cells A_i ×ˢ A_j where A_i = disjointed B i\n643 |       have hCover : (Set.univ : Set (X × X)) =\n644 |           ⋃ (ij : Fin n × Fin n), disjointed B ij.1 ×ˢ disjointed B ij.2 := by\n645 |         rw [Set.iUnion_prod]\n646 |         have hU : (⋃ i, disjointed B i) = (Set.univ : Set X) :=\n647 |           (Set.subset_univ _).antisymm hA_cover\n648 |         simp only [hU, Set.univ_prod_univ]\n649 |       have hDisj : Pairwise (Disjoint on\n650 |           fun ij : Fin n × Fin n => disjointed B ij.1 ×ˢ disjointed B ij.2) := by\n651 |         intro ij ij' hij\n652 |         simp only [Function.onFun]\n653 |         rw [Set.disjoint_prod]\n654 |         by_cases h1 : ij.1 = ij'.1\n655 |         · right; exact hA_disjoint (fun heq => hij (Prod.ext h1 heq))\n656 |         · left; exact hA_disjoint h1\n657 |       have hMeas' : ∀ (ij : Fin n × Fin n),\n658 |           MeasurableSet (disjointed B ij.1 ×ˢ disjointed B ij.2) :=\n659 |         fun ij => MeasurableSet.prod (hA_meas ij.1) (hA_meas ij.2)\n\n... 885 more line(s) below (use offset=660 to continue reading)\nTime: 2026-08-10 01:20:18 UTC","timestamp":1786324818,"token_count":2166,"tool_call_id":"31205584-3c5f-43aa-8a86-4add5ac4ae41","full_token_estimate":1436,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/PositiveDefiniteIntegral.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":1544,"byte_count":80022,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":49,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":68,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1786324946,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\":\"interface_boltzmann_character_expansion\",\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/PeterWeyl.lean\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"4fce178e-18a6-4804-bc9a-8d32328858f4","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1097,"reasoning_content":"This is very helpful! The `PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg` lemma gives exactly what I need:\n\n```\nFor a Mercer-PD kernel K : X → X → ℂ (continuous), and a continuous function f,\n∫∫ f(x) * conj(f(y)) * K(x, y) dμ dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nThis is the KEY lemma for the PD step. If I can show that the interface kernel `K(U⁺, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` is Mercer-PD for each fixed u⁰, then I can apply this lemma to get non-negativity for each u⁰.\n\nBut as I analyzed, the character expansion gives `Ψ_w(u⁰)` which is not non-negative, so the kernel is NOT Mercer-PD for each fixed u⁰.\n\nWait, but maybe I'm wrong about this. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe Mercer-PD of the kernel `K(U⁺, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` means:\n```\n∑_{i,j} c_i * conj(c_j) * K(U⁺_i, W⁺_j) ≥ 0\n```\n\nfor all finite sets and coefficients. This is equivalent to the character expansion giving non-negative coefficients:\n```\nK(U⁺, W⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Φ_w(U⁺) · conj(Φ_w(W⁺))\n```\n\nFor this to be Mercer-PD, we need `F(w) · Ψ_w(u⁰) ≥ 0` for all w. But `Ψ_w(u⁰)` is a product of characters, which is generally complex. So the kernel is NOT Mercer-PD for each fixed u⁰.\n\nSo the design doc's claim is WRONG: the kernel is NOT PD for each fixed u⁰.\n\nBUT, the full integral (including the u⁰ integration) IS ≥ 0. So the non-negativity comes from the u⁰ integration.\n\nHmm, but how? If the kernel is not PD for each u⁰, then `∫∫ H(U⁺) · conj(H(W⁺)) · K(U⁺, W⁺) dμ⁺ dμ⁺` is NOT ≥ 0 for each u⁰. So integrating over u⁰ doesn't help (the integral of a function that's sometimes negative can be negative).\n\nWait, but the Osterwalder-Seiler theorem says the full integral IS ≥ 0. So there must be some mechanism that makes it work.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the correct approach is NOT \"PD for each u⁰\" but rather \"PD on the combined space (U⁺, u⁰) × (W⁺, u⁰)\".\n\nThe kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` is a function of THREE variables. If we view it as a kernel on the space `X = (U⁺, u⁰)` × `Y = (W⁺, u⁰)`, then it's `K((U⁺, u⁰), (W⁺, u⁰))`. But this is a kernel on `X × X` where `X = (U⁺, u⁰)`, and the kernel is `K((U⁺, u⁰), (W⁺, u⁰')) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` which doesn't depend on `u⁰'`.\n\nFor this to be Mercer-PD on `X = (U⁺, u⁰)`, we need:\n```\n∑_{i,j} c_i * conj(c_j) * K((U⁺_i, u⁰_i), (W⁺_j, u⁰_j)) ≥ 0\n```\n\ni.e., `∑_{i,j} c_i * conj(c_j) * exp(-β S_int(U⁺_i, u⁰_i, θW⁺_j)) ≥ 0`.\n\nThe character expansion gives:\n```\n= ∑_w F(w) · [∑_i c_i · Φ_w(U⁺_i) · Ψ_w(u⁰_i)] · conj(∑_j c_j · Φ_w(W⁺_j))\n```\n\nWait, this doesn't factor nicely because `Ψ_w(u⁰_i)` is inside the sum over `i` but `conj(Φ_w(W⁺_j))` is inside the sum over `j`. Let me redo this.\n\n```\n∑_{i,j} c_i * conj(c_j) * K((U⁺_i, u⁰_i), (W⁺_j, u⁰_j))\n= ∑_{i,j} c_i * conj(c_j) * ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺_i) · Ψ_w(u⁰_i) · conj(Φ_w(W⁺_j))\n= ∑_w F(w) · [∑_i c_i · Φ_w(U⁺_i) · Ψ_w(u⁰_i)] · conj(∑_j c_j · Φ_w(W⁺_j))\n```\n\nHmm, this is `∑_w F(w) · A_w · conj(B_w)` where `A_w = ∑_i c_i · Φ_w(U⁺_i) · Ψ_w(u⁰_i)` and `B_w = ∑_j c_j · Φ_w(W⁺_j)`. This is NOT `∑_w F(w) · |A_w|²` because `A_w ≠ B_w` (A_w has the extra `Ψ_w(u⁰_i)` factor).\n\nSo this is NOT Mercer-PD either. The kernel `K((U⁺, u⁰), (W⁺, u⁰'))` is NOT Mercer-PD on `X = (U⁺, u⁰)`.\n\nOK so I'm really stuck on the mathematics. Let me reconsider the whole approach.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the structure of the interface action. Maybe the interface links `u⁰` don't appear in the same way I think. Let me look at the actual plaquette structure.\n\nIn the Osterwalder-Seiler setup, the interface plaquettes are those that cross the t=0 plane. Each such plaquette has links at t=0 (interface) and links at t=±1 (positive/negative). The key point is that the interface links appear in the plaquette as `g₃⁻¹` or `g₄⁻¹` (inverted), and the reflection inverts the temporal links.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is about which links are \"U⁺ links\", \"u⁰ links\", and \"V⁺ links\" in the character expansion. Let me look at the `interface_boltzmann_character_expansion` lemma to understand the partition.\n\nLet me search for it.","turn":51,"is_prune_marker":false}
