{"id":73,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 2947 lines, 172452 bytes --\n1430 | with `interface_product_character_expansion` (∏_p exp(c·Re Tr(...)) = ∑_w F(w)·Φ_w·Ψ_w·V_w),\n1431 | the interface Boltzmann factor admits the character expansion (viewed in ℂ)\n1432 | \n1433 |     (exp(-β·S_int(U)) : ℂ) = (C : ℂ) · ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U) · Ψ_w(U) · V_w(U)\n1434 | \n1435 | with C > 0 and F(w) ≥ 0, where Φ_w(U) = ∏_{l ∈ L_U} χ_{w(l)}(g_l),\n1436 | Ψ_w(U) = ∏_{l ∈ L_0} χ_{w(l)}(g_l), V_w(U) = star(∏_{l ∈ L_V} χ_{dual(w(l))}(g_l)),\n1437 | and g_l = interfaceLinkVar U l.  This is step 3 of sub-step (iii) of Lemma 2\n1438 | (`transfer_matrix_integral_reduction`): substituting the character expansion\n1439 | into the transfer matrix inner product. Uses `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette`\n1440 | (axiom count 6, unchanged); 0 sorries. -/\n1441 | lemma interface_boltzmann_character_expansion (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n1442 |     (hN : 1 ≤ N) :\n1443 |     ∃ (C : ℝ) (hC : 0 < C)\n1444 |       (ι : Type) (hι : Fintype ι) (dims : ι → ℕ)\n1445 |       (ρ : ∀ i, SU N →* Matrix (Fin (dims i)) (Fin (dims i)) ℂ)\n1446 |       (hU : ∀ i, IsUnitaryRepresentation (ρ i))\n1447 |       (hMeas : ∀ i, Measurable (repCharacter (ρ i)))\n1448 |       (dual : ι → ι)\n1449 |       (F : (InterfaceLink T L → ι) → ℝ) (hF : ∀ w, 0 ≤ F w),\n1450 |       ∀ (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)),\n1451 |       (Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U) : ℂ) =\n1452 |         (C : ℂ) * ∑ w : InterfaceLink T L → ι, (F w : ℂ) *\n1453 |           (∏ l ∈ interfaceLinkPos T L, repCharacter (ρ (w l)) (interfaceLinkVar N T L U l)) *\n1454 |           (∏ l ∈ interfaceLinkInt T L, repCharacter (ρ (w l)) (interfaceLinkVar N T L U l)) *\n1455 |           star (∏ l ∈ interfaceLinkNeg T L, repCharacter (ρ (dual (w l))) (interfaceLinkVar N T L U l)) := by\n1456 |   -- C = ∏_p exp(-β²) is independent of U; obtain it once (uniform abstract-product form).\n1457 |   obtain ⟨C, hC, hC_eq_all⟩ := interface_boltzmann_eq_abstract_product N T L β\n1458 |   -- The character-expansion data (ι, ρ, dual, F) is independent of U (uniform version).\n1459 |   obtain ⟨ι, hι, dims, ρ, hU, hMeas, dual, F, hF, hF_decomp⟩ :=\n1460 |     interface_product_character_expansion N T L β hN\n1461 |   letI : Fintype ι := hι\n1462 |   classical\n1463 |   refine ⟨C, hC, ι, hι, dims, ρ, hU, hMeas, dual, F, hF, fun U => ?_⟩\n1464 |   -- Per-U: combine the abstract-product form with the character expansion.\n1465 |   rw [hC_eq_all U]\n1466 |   have h := hF_decomp U\n1467 |   norm_cast at h\n1468 |   rw [Complex.ofReal_mul, h]\n1469 | \n1470 | #print axioms interface_boltzmann_character_expansion\n1471 | \n1472 | /-! ### Bridge lemmas: link partition ↔ site partition\n1473 | \n1474 | These lemmas connect the LINK-based partition (`interfaceLinkPos/Int/Neg`, used by\n1475 | the character expansion) with the SITE-based partition\n1476 | (`positiveSites/interfaceSites/negativeSites`, used by the measure factorization\n1477 | in `TransferMatrix.lean`).  A link `(n, μ)` is in `interfaceLinkPos` iff its base\n1478 | site `n` is in `positiveSites`, etc.  This compatibility is needed for sub-step\n1479 | (iii) of Lemma 2 (Fubini reduction).  All 0 sorries, 0 custom axioms. -/\n1480 | \n1481 | lemma interfaceLinkPos_mem_iff (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n1482 |     (l : InterfaceLink T L) :\n1483 |     l ∈ interfaceLinkPos T L ↔ l.val.1 ∈ positiveSites T L := by\n1484 |   simp only [interfaceLinkPos, Finset.mem_filter, Finset.mem_univ, true_and,\n1485 |     positiveSites]\n1486 | \n1487 | lemma interfaceLinkInt_mem_iff (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n1488 |     (l : InterfaceLink T L) :\n1489 |     l ∈ interfaceLinkInt T L ↔ l.val.1 ∈ interfaceSites T L := by\n1490 |   simp only [interfaceLinkInt, Finset.mem_filter, Finset.mem_univ, true_and,\n1491 |     interfaceSites]\n1492 | \n1493 | lemma interfaceLinkNeg_mem_iff (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n1494 |     (l : InterfaceLink T L) :\n1495 |     l ∈ interfaceLinkNeg T L ↔ l.val.1 ∈ negativeSites T L := by\n1496 |   simp only [interfaceLinkNeg, Finset.mem_filter, Finset.mem_univ, true_and,\n1497 |     negativeSites]\n1498 | \n1499 | #print axioms interfaceLinkPos_mem_iff\n1500 | #print axioms interfaceLinkInt_mem_iff\n1501 | #print axioms interfaceLinkNeg_mem_iff\n1502 | \n1503 | \n1504 | lemma reflectSite_addVector_comm (T L : ℕ) (n : PeriodicSite T L) (μ : Fin 4) (hμ0 : μ ≠ 0) :\n1505 |     ReflectSite.reflectSite (AddVector.addVector n μ) =\n1506 |     AddVector.addVector (ReflectSite.reflectSite n) μ := by\n1507 |   -- For spatial directions (μ ≠ 0), reflection commutes with adding a basis vector\n1508 |   fin_cases μ\n1509 |   · -- μ = 0 is excluded by hμ0\n1510 |     exfalso; exact hμ0 rfl\n1511 |   · -- μ = 1 (x direction): adding e_x doesn't change time, so reflection commutes\n1512 |     ext <;> simp [ReflectSite.reflectSite, AddVector.addVector, reflectSitePeriodic, addVectorPeriodic]\n1513 |   · -- μ = 2 (y direction)\n1514 |     ext <;> simp [ReflectSite.reflectSite, AddVector.addVector, reflectSitePeriodic, addVectorPeriodic]\n1515 |   · -- μ = 3 (z direction)\n1516 |     ext <;> simp [ReflectSite.reflectSite, AddVector.addVector, reflectSitePeriodic, addVectorPeriodic]\n1517 | \n1518 | /-- For spatial directions (μ ≠ 0), addition and reflection commute at the level of\n1519 |     the reflected site applied twice. -/\n\n... 1428 more line(s) below (use offset=1520 to continue reading)\nTime: 2026-08-10 01:26:18 UTC","timestamp":1786325178,"token_count":1698,"tool_call_id":"e331940a-d2cd-4ab1-bb23-c893fac7591b","full_token_estimate":1130,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":2947,"byte_count":172452,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":55,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":74,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1786325359,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"docs\",\"pattern\":\"8.11.58\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"5647f519-e476-4a6e-99af-931338057d65","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1865,"reasoning_content":"Now I understand the structure better. The interface Boltzmann factor character expansion is:\n```\nexp(-β S_int(U)) = C · ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U) · Ψ_w(U) · V_w(U)\n```\n\nwhere:\n- `Φ_w(U) = ∏_{l ∈ interfaceLinkPos} χ_{w(l)}(interfaceLinkVar U l)` — positive links\n- `Ψ_w(U) = ∏_{l ∈ interfaceLinkInt} χ_{w(l)}(interfaceLinkVar U l)` — interface links\n- `V_w(U) = star(∏_{l ∈ interfaceLinkNeg} χ_{dual(w(l))}(interfaceLinkVar U l))` — negative links (conjugated dual)\n\nSo the interface links `u⁰` appear in `Ψ_w(U)`. And the negative links appear with conjugated dual characters.\n\nNow, the key question is: after the change of variables `U⁻ → W⁺ = θU⁻`, the negative links become the reflected positive links. The `V_w` factor involves `interfaceLinkNeg` links, which after the change of variables become the reflected `W⁺` links.\n\nSo the kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` has the expansion:\n```\nK(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = C · ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(W⁺))\n```\n\nwhere `Φ_w(U⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ L_pos} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`, `Ψ_w(u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ L_int} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)`, and `conj(Φ_w(W⁺)) = conj(∏_{l ∈ L_neg} χ_{dual(w(l))}(g_l))`.\n\nWait, but after the change of variables, the negative links are `θW⁺`. The `V_w` factor involves `interfaceLinkNeg` links of the ORIGINAL config `U`. After the change of variables `U⁻ → W⁺ = θU⁻`, the negative links become `θW⁺` (the reflection of W⁺). So `V_w` involves the characters of the `θW⁺` links.\n\nNow, the reflection `θ` inverts temporal links and keeps spatial links. So `interfaceLinkVar(θW⁺) l` for a negative link `l` is either the inverse (if temporal) or the same (if spatial) of the corresponding W⁺ link.\n\nThe character of the inverse is `χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g))` (for unitary representations). And the character of the same is `χ(g)`.\n\nSo `V_w(θW⁺) = star(∏_{l ∈ L_neg} χ_{dual(w(l))}(interfaceLinkVar(θW⁺) l))`.\n\nFor temporal negative links, `interfaceLinkVar(θW⁺) l = (interfaceLinkVar W⁺ l')⁻¹` for some corresponding positive link `l'`. So `χ_{dual(w(l))}(g⁻¹) = conj(χ_{dual(w(l))}(g)) = conj(conj(χ_{w(l)}(g))) = χ_{w(l)}(g)` (using `χ_{dual}(g) = conj(χ(g))` and `χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g))`).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, I think the key point is that the `V_w` factor, after the change of variables, becomes `conj(Φ_w(W⁺))` — the conjugate of the same character product that appears for U⁺. This is what makes the kernel a sum of rank-1 terms `Φ_w(U⁺) · conj(Φ_w(W⁺))`, which is the Mercer-PD structure.\n\nBut the `Ψ_w(u⁰)` factor is still there, and it's not non-negative. So the kernel is:\n```\nK(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = C · ∑_w F(w) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Φ_w(U⁺) · conj(Φ_w(W⁺))\n```\n\nFor this to be Mercer-PD in (U⁺, W⁺) for each u⁰, we need `F(w) · Ψ_w(u⁰) ≥ 0`. But `Ψ_w(u⁰)` is complex. So it's NOT Mercer-PD for each u⁰.\n\nOK so I'm now fairly convinced that the \"PD for each u⁰\" claim in the design doc is WRONG. The correct argument must involve the u⁰ integration.\n\nLet me think about what the correct argument is. The full integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(H(W⁺, u⁰_s)) · K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) dμ⁺ dμ⁺ dμ⁰\n= C · ∑_w F(w) ∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · |∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺|² dμ⁰\n```\n\nNow, `Ψ_w(u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ L_int} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)` where `L_int` is the set of interface links. The integral over u⁰ involves all interface link variables.\n\nThe `|∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺|²` depends on u⁰_s (spatial interface links) but not u⁰_t (temporal interface links).\n\nSo:\n```\n∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · |...|² dμ⁰ = ∫_{u⁰_s} [∫_{u⁰_t} Ψ_w(u⁰_s, u⁰_t) dμ⁰_t] · |...(u⁰_s)|² dμ⁰_s\n```\n\nThe inner integral `∫_{u⁰_t} ∏_{l ∈ L_int_t} χ_{w(l)}(g_l) dμ⁰_t` is 0 unless all temporal interface characters are trivial (Schur orthogonality), in which case it's 1.\n\nFor the surviving terms (temporal interface characters all trivial):\n```\n= ∫_{u⁰_s} [∏_{l ∈ L_int_s} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · |∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺|² dμ⁰_s\n```\n\nThis is `∫ χ(g) · |F(g)|² dμ(g)` where `χ` is a product of characters and `F ≥ 0`. This is NOT necessarily non-negative.\n\nSo even after integrating over u⁰_t, the result is not necessarily non-negative. The spatial interface links still cause problems.\n\nHmm, but wait. Maybe I need to also expand `H` in characters of the spatial interface links. If `H(U⁺, u⁰_s) = f(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` is expanded in characters of the u⁰_s links, then the character orthogonality on u⁰_s would also select specific terms.\n\nLet me think about this. If we expand `H(U⁺, u⁰_s) = ∑_v a_v(u⁰_s) · Φ_v(U⁺)` (expanding in characters of U⁺ links), then:\n```\n∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺ = a_w(u⁰_s)\n```\n\n(by character orthogonality on U⁺ links). So:\n```\n|∫ H(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_w(U⁺) dμ⁺|² = |a_w(u⁰_s)|²\n```\n\nAnd:\n```\nI = C · ∑_w F(w) ∫_{u⁰_s} [∏_{l ∈ L_int_s} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · |a_w(u⁰_s)|² dμ⁰_s\n```\n\nNow, `a_w(u⁰_s)` is a function of the spatial interface links. If we further expand `a_w(u⁰_s) = ∑_v b_{w,v} · Ψ_v(u⁰_s)` (in characters of u⁰_s links), then:\n```\n|a_w(u⁰_s)|² = |∑_v b_{w,v} · Ψ_v(u⁰_s)|²\n```\n\nAnd:\n```\n∫_{u⁰_s} [∏_{l ∈ L_int_s} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)] · |a_w(u⁰_s)|² dμ⁰_s\n= ∫_{u⁰_s} Ψ_w(u⁰_s) · |a_w(u⁰_s)|² dμ⁰_s\n```\n\nThis is `∫ Ψ_w · |a_w|² dμ`. If `a_w = ∑_v b_{w,v} Ψ_v`, then `|a_w|² = ∑_{v,v'} b_{w,v} conj(b_{w,v'}) Ψ_v conj(Ψ_{v'})`. And:\n```\n∫ Ψ_w · Ψ_v · conj(Ψ_{v'}) dμ = ∫ χ_{w} · χ_{v} · conj(χ_{v'}) dμ\n```\n\nBy character orthogonality (triple product), this is related to Clebsch-Gordan coefficients. Specifically, `∫ χ_w · χ_v · conj(χ_{v'}) dμ = N_{w,v}^{v'}` (the multiplicity of `v'` in `w ⊗ v`), which is a non-negative integer.\n\nSo:\n```\n∫ Ψ_w · |a_w|² dμ = ∑_{v,v'} b_{w,v} · conj(b_{w,v'}) · N_{w,v}^{v'}\n```\n\nThis is a quadratic form in `b_{w,v}` with coefficients `N_{w,v}^{v'}` (Clebsch-Gordan multiplicities). Is this non-negative?\n\nActually, `N_{w,v}^{v'}` is the multiplicity of `v'` in `w ⊗ v`. The integral `∫ χ_w · χ_v · conj(χ_{v'}) dμ = N_{w,v}^{v'}`. And:\n```\n∫ Ψ_w · |a_w|² dμ = ∫ χ_w · |∑_v b_v χ_v|² dμ = ∑_{v,v'} b_v conj(b_{v'}) ∫ χ_w χ_v conj(χ_{v'}) dμ = ∑_{v,v'} b_v conj(b_{v'}) N_{w,v}^{v'}\n```\n\nHmm, this is `∑_v b_v (∑_{v'} N_{w,v}^{v'} conj(b_{v'}))`. The matrix `M_{v,v'} = N_{w,v}^{v'}` is the Clebsch-Gordan multiplicity matrix. Is this PSD?\n\nActually, `N_{w,v}^{v'} = ∫ χ_w(g) χ_v(g) conj(χ_{v'}(g)) dμ(g)`. And:\n```\n∑_{v,v'} b_v conj(b_{v'}) N_{w,v}^{v'} = ∫ χ_w(g) |∑_v b_v χ_v(g)|² dμ(g) = ∫ χ_w(g) |a(g)|² dμ(g)\n```\n\nThis is `∫ χ_w · |a|² dμ`. For this to be ≥ 0, we need `χ_w` to be... well, `χ_w` is a character, which is NOT necessarily non-negative. So this is NOT necessarily ≥ 0.\n\nWait, but `χ_w(g) = Tr(ρ_w(g))`. And `|a(g)|² ≥ 0`. So `∫ χ_w(g) |a(g)|² dμ(g) = ∫ Tr(ρ_w(g)) |a(g)|² dμ(g)`. This is the integral of a character times a non-negative function. This is NOT necessarily non-negative.\n\nSo I'm STILL stuck. The character expansion approach does NOT give non-negativity, even after integrating over all interface links.\n\nOK, I think I need to fundamentally reconsider the approach. Let me re-read the §8.11.58 analysis to understand what was found.\n\nActually, let me re-read the §8.11.58 section. The handoff says \"the matrix M is NOT PSD (U(1) counterexample)\". Let me find and read this section.","turn":57,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":75,"role":"Tool","content":"Searched for \"8.11.58\" in C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\n4 match(es):\ntransfer_matrix_positivity_design.md:5427: ### 8.11.58 CRITICAL FINDING: The triple-product matrix M is NOT PSD — the step 5 approach does NOT work as stated; the gauge invariance hypothesis is likely needed (2026-08-09 session 71)\ntransfer_matrix_positivity_design.md:5517: This session resolved the key open question from §8.11.58: **the gauge invariance hypothesis is NOT needed, and the §8.11.53 resolution is CORRECT.** The axiom `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` is TRUE for ALL `f` with `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface` (not just gauge-invariant `f`). The §8.11.58 finding (that the matrix `J_l` is NOT PSD) is CORRECT but only shows that the step 5 approach (triple product → PSD Gram) is the wrong PROOF STRATEGY — it does NOT show the axiom is false. The correct proof uses a DIFFERENT mechanism: the reflection positivity via the PD of the interface kernel, combined with a change of variables that resolves the `S_pos`/`S_neg` asymmetry.\ntransfer_matrix_positivity_design.md:5540: The apparent obstacle (noted in §8.11.58 analysis) is the asymmetry between `exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` (on the U⁺ side) and `exp(-β S_neg(V⁺))` (on the V⁺ side): the PD of the interface kernel requires the SAME function `H` on both sides, but `S_pos ≠ S_neg` as functions.\ntransfer_matrix_positivity_design.md:5628: | Does the step 5 approach (triple product → PSD Gram) work? | **NO** (the matrix `J_l` is NOT PSD, as §8.11.58 showed) |\nTime: 2026-08-10 01:29:19 UTC","timestamp":1786325359,"token_count":468,"tool_call_id":"5647f519-e476-4a6e-99af-931338057d65","full_token_estimate":335,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"grep","file_path":"docs","old_text":"8.11.58","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":4,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":50,"edit_line":null},"turn":57,"is_prune_marker":false}
