tte_p))` is a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors.\n3. Each plaquette Boltzmann factor `exp(-β Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃ g₄))` is PD on `SU(N)⁴` (as a group).\n4. The product of PD functions on overlapping variables is PD on the product group.\n5. The reflection positivity follows from the PD of the Boltzmann factor.\n\nStep 5 is the key. The Osterwalder-Seiler proof uses the fact that the reflection `θ` is an involution that maps the positive half to the negative half. The reflection positivity integral is:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nThe key insight (which I think I was missing) is that the reflection positivity can be reduced to the GROUP PD by a suitable identification.\n\nSpecifically, the reflection `θ` maps the full config `U` to `θU`. The positive half of `θU` is `θ(U⁻)` (the reflected negative half). So `F(θU⁺) = F(θ(U⁻))`.\n\nNow, the group PD of `exp(-β S)` means:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is a SINGLE integral (not a double integral). But we can write it as:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θ(U⁻))) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nNow, the key: `exp(-β S(U)) = exp(-β S(U⁻¹ · θU))`? No, that's not true in general.\n\nHmm, actually, let me think about this differently. The reflection `θ` is an involution. The reflection positivity integral is:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nLet me substitute `U → θU` (measure-preserving):\n```\nI = ∫ F((θU)⁺) conj(F(U⁺)) exp(-β S(θU)) dμ(U) = ∫ F(θ(U⁻)) conj(F(U⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\n(using `S(θU) = S(U)`). So `I = conj(I)` (since `I` is real, this is consistent).\n\nNow, the group PD integral is:\n```\nJ = ∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nIf I set `G(U) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2)` and `V = θU`, then:\n```\nJ' = ∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ θU)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nBut `U⁻¹ θU` is NOT the same as `U` (or `θU`). So `exp(-β S(U⁻¹ θU)) ≠ exp(-β S(U))`.\n\nSo this approach doesn't work directly.\n\nLet me think about the Osterwalder-Seiler proof from a different angle. I think the key is that the Osterwalder-Seiler proof does NOT use the group PD of the FULL Boltzmann factor. Instead, it uses the PD of the INTERFACE kernel, but in a specific way that accounts for the reflection.\n\nActually, I think the correct approach is the following:\n\nThe interface kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` is a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors. Each plaquette Boltzmann factor `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` is PD on `SU(N)⁴`.\n\nThe key insight is that the plaquette Boltzmann factor, when viewed as a function of (g₁, g₂) on the \"left\" and (g₃, g₄) on the \"right\", is a PD KERNEL (not a PD function). Specifically:\n```\nB_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))\n```\n\nThis can be written as `φ(g₁ g₂ · (g₃ g₄)⁻¹)` where `φ(x) = exp(c · Re Tr(x))` is PD on `SU(N)`. Wait, no — `g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹ ≠ g₁ g₂ · (g₃ g₄)⁻¹` in general (matrix multiplication is not commutative).\n\nHmm, but `g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹ = (g₁ g₂) · (g₃ g₄)⁻¹` only if `g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹ = (g₃ g₄)⁻¹ = g₄⁻¹ g₃⁻¹`, which requires `g₃` and `g₄` to commute. So this doesn't work in general.\n\nOK, let me think about this differently. The plaquette Boltzmann factor `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` is PD on `SU(N)⁴` (as a group). The PD means:\n```\n∑_{i,j} c_i conj(c_j) B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) ≥ 0\n```\n\nwhere `g_i = (g₁_i, g₂_i, g₃_i, g₄_i)` and `g_i⁻¹ g_j = (g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j, g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j, g₃_i⁻¹ g₃_j, g₄_i⁻¹ g₄_j)`.\n\nNow, `B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j · g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j · (g₃_i⁻¹ g₃_j)⁻¹ · (g₄_i⁻¹ g₄_j)⁻¹))` = `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j · g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j · g₃_j⁻¹ g₃_i · g₄_j⁻¹ g₄_i))`.\n\nThis is the GROUP PD, which involves `g_i⁻¹ g_j`. The reflection positivity involves `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄)` (not `B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j)`).\n\nThe connection between the two is through the REFLECTION. The reflection maps the negative links to the positive links with inversion on temporal links. So if we identify the \"left\" variables with the positive links and the \"right\" variables with the reflected negative links, the group PD `B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j)` might match the reflection positivity `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄)`.\n\nSpecifically, if we set `g₁_j = g₁_i · g₁` (i.e., `g₁ = g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j`), `g₂_j = g₂_i · g₂`, `g₃_j = g₃_i · g₃⁻¹` (note the inverse!), `g₄_j = g₄_i · g₄⁻¹` (note the inverse!), then:\n```\nB_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ · g₂ · (g₃⁻¹)⁻¹ · (g₄⁻¹)⁻¹)) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃ g₄))\n```\n\nWait, that gives `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃ g₄))`, not `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))`. Let me redo this.\n\n`B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))`.\n\n`B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr((g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j) (g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j) (g₃_i⁻¹ g₃_j)⁻¹ (g₄_i⁻¹ g₄_j)⁻¹))` = `exp(c · Re Tr((g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j) (g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j) (g₃_j⁻¹ g₃_i) (g₄_j⁻¹ g₄_i)))`.\n\nIf I set `g₁_j = g₁_i · a`, `g₂_j = g₂_i · b`, `g₃_j = g₃_i · c⁻¹`, `g₄_j = g₄_i · d⁻¹`, then:\n```\nB_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr(a · b · (c⁻¹)⁻¹ · (d⁻¹)⁻¹)) = exp(c · Re Tr(a · b · c · d))\n```\n\nHmm, that gives `exp(c · Re Tr(a b c d))`, not `exp(c · Re Tr(a b c⁻¹ d⁻¹))`.\n\nLet me try: `g₃_j = g₃_i · c`, `g₄_j = g₄_i · d`:\n```\nB_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr(a · b · c⁻¹ · d⁻¹))\n```\n\nYes! So if `g₁_j = g₁_i · a`, `g₂_j = g₂_i · b`, `g₃_j = g₃_i · c`, `g₄_j = g₄_i · d`, then `B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr(a b c⁻¹ d⁻¹)) = B_p(a, b, c, d)`.\n\nSo the group PD `∑_{i,j} c_i conj(c_j) B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) ≥ 0` becomes `∑_{i,j} c_i conj(c_j) B_p(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)⁻¹ ... ` hmm, this isn't quite right because `a, b, c, d` depend on both `i` and `j`.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The group PD is:\n```\n∑_{i,j} c_i conj(c_j) B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) ≥ 0\n```\n\nwhere `g_i = (g₁_i, g₂_i, g₃_i, g₄_i)` and `g_i⁻¹ g_j = (g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j, g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j, g₃_i⁻¹ g₃_j, g₄_i⁻¹ g₄_j)`.\n\nSo `B_p(g_i⁻¹ g_j) = exp(c · Re Tr((g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j)(g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j)(g₃_i⁻¹ g₃_j)⁻¹(g₄_i⁻¹ g₄_j)⁻¹))`.\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral (for a single plaquette) is:\n```\nI_p = ∫ F(g₁, g₂) conj(F(W₃, W₄)) B_p(g₁, g₂, W₃, W₄) dμ(g₁) dμ(g₂) dμ(W₃) dμ(W₄)\n```\n\nWait, but `B_p(g₁, g₂, W₃, W₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ W₃⁻¹ W₄⁻¹))` (the plaquette involves g₃⁻¹ and g₄⁻¹, and after the change of variables, g₃ = W₃ (temporal, reflected = inverted, so g₃ = W₃⁻¹, and g₃⁻¹ = W₃) — wait, I need to be more careful.\n\nActually, I realize I've been going back and forth on the plaquette structure. Let me be very precise.\n\nThe plaquette Boltzmann factor for a plaquette with links g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄ is:\n```\nB_p = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))\n```\n\nThis is the STANDARD Wilson plaquette (oriented around the plaquette). The links g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄ are the four links around the plaquette, with g₃ and g₄ being the \"return\" links (hence the inverses).\n\nNow, for an interface plaquette, the links come from different halves:\n- g₁, g₂ might be from U⁺ (positive) or u⁰ (interface)\n- g₃, g₄ might be from U⁻ (negative) or u⁰ (interface)\n\nAfter the change of variables `U⁻ → W⁺ = θU⁻`:\n- A negative temporal link g₃ becomes W₃ = g₃⁻¹ (reflected = inverted). So g₃ = W₃⁻¹, and g₃⁻¹ = W₃.\n- A negative spatial link g₄ becomes W₄ = g₄ (reflected = not inverted). So g₄ = W₄, and g₄⁻¹ = W₄⁻¹.\n\nSo the plaquette Boltzmann factor becomes:\n```\nB_p = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ W₃ W₄⁻¹))\n```\n\nwhere g₁, g₂ are from U⁺/u⁰, W₃ is from W⁺ (temporal), and W₄ is from W⁺ (spatial).\n\nNow, the group PD of `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` on `SU(N)⁴` means:\n```\n∑_{i,j} c_i conj(c_j) exp(c · Re Tr((g₁_i⁻¹ g₁_j)(g₂_i⁻¹ g₂_j)(g₃_i⁻¹ g₃_j)⁻¹(g₄_i⁻¹ g₄_j)⁻¹)) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThe reflection positivity integral (for a single plaquette, no interface links) is:\n```\nI_p = ∫ F(g₁, g₂) conj(F(W₃, W₄)) exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ W₃ W₄⁻¹)) dμ dμ dμ dμ\n```\n\nNow, the group PD integral (continuous version) is:\n```\nJ = ∫∫ G(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) conj(G(h₁, h₂, h₃, h₄)) exp(c · Re Tr((g₁⁻¹h₁)(g₂⁻¹h₂)(g₃⁻¹h₃)⁻¹(g₄⁻¹h₄)⁻¹)) dμ dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nSimplifying: `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁⁻¹h₁ · g₂⁻¹h₂ · h₃⁻¹g₃ · h₄⁻¹g₄))`.\n\nNow, if I set `G(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = F(g₁, g₂) · δ(g₃ = 1) · δ(g₄ = 1)` (i.e., G is supported on g₃ = g₄ = 1), then:\n```\nJ = ∫∫ F(g₁, g₂) conj(F(h₁, h₂)) exp(c · Re Tr(g₁⁻¹h₁ · g₂⁻¹h₂ · h₃⁻¹ · h₄⁻¹)) dμ(g₁) dμ(g₂) dμ(h₁) dμ(h₂)\n```\n\nHmm, this has `h₃⁻¹` and `h₄⁻¹` but we set g₃ = g₄ = 1, so `(g₃⁻¹h₃)⁻¹ = h₃⁻¹` and `(g₄⁻¹h₄)⁻¹ = h₄⁻¹`. So:\n```\nJ = ∫∫ F(g₁, g₂) conj(F(h₁, h₂)) exp(c · Re Tr(g₁⁻¹h₁ · g₂⁻¹h₂ · h₃⁻¹ · h₄⁻¹)) dμ dμ\n```\n\nBut this is a 6-fold integral (g₁, g₂, h₁, h₂, h₃, h₄), not a 4-fold integral. And it has `h₃⁻¹ · h₄⁻¹` which are extra variables.\n\nThis doesn't match the reflection positivity integral `I_p = ∫ F(g₁, g₂) conj(F(W₃, W₄)) exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ W₃ W₄⁻¹)) dμ dμ dμ dμ`.\n\nThe issue is that the group PD integral has `g⁻¹ h` (the group product), while the reflection positivity has `g₁ g₂ W₃ W₄⁻¹` (a specific product of 4 variables, not a group product of 2 configs).\n\nSo the group PD of the plaquette Boltzmann factor does NOT directly give the reflection positivity. The connection is more subtle.\n\nOK, I think I need to look at this from the perspective of the CHARACTER EXPANSION. The character expansion of `exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` gives:\n```\nexp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹)) = ∑_{r,s,t,u,v} coeff(r,s,t,u,v) · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · χ_u(g₃⁻¹) · χ_v(g₄⁻¹)\n= ∑_{r,s,t,u,v} coeff(r,s,t,u,v) · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · conj(χ_u(g₃)) · conj(χ_v(g₄))\n```\n\nAfter the change of variables (g₃ = W₃⁻¹ temporal, g₄ = W₄ spatial):\n```\nexp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ W₃ W₄⁻¹)) = ∑_{r,s,t,u,v} coeff(r,s,t,u,v) · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · conj(χ_u(W₃⁻¹)) · conj(χ_v(W₄))\n= ∑_{r,s,t,u,v} coeff(r,s,t,u,v) · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · χ_u(W₃) · conj(χ_v(W₄))\n```\n\n(using `conj(χ_u(W₃⁻¹)) = conj(conj(χ_u(W₃))) = χ_u(W₃)`).\n\nSo the kernel is:\n```\nK(g₁, g₂, W₃, W₄) = ∑_{r,s,t,u,v} coeff(r,s,t,u,v) · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · χ_u(W₃) · conj(χ_v(W₄))\n```\n\nThe W₃ factor is NOT conjugated (because the reflection inversion cancels the plaquette inversion), and the W₄ factor IS conjugated (because the spatial reflection doesn't cancel the plaquette inversion).\n\nFor the Mercer-PD, we need ALL W⁺ factors to be conjugated. But W₃ is NOT conjugated. So the kernel is NOT Mercer-PD.\n\nHOWEVER, if we group the W⁺ factors differently — specifically, if we put W₃ with the U⁺ factors (g₁, g₂) and W₄ with the conjugated factors — then the kernel becomes:\n```\nK = ∑_{r,s,t,u,v} coeff(r,s,t,u,v) · [χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · χ_u(W₃)] · conj(χ_v(W₄))\n```\n\nThis is a sum of rank-1 terms `Φ(g₁, g₂, W₃) · conj(Ψ(W₄))` weighted by `coeff ≥ 0`. But this is a kernel on (g₁, g₂, W₃) × (W₄), not on (g₁, g₂) × (W₃, W₄). So it's not the right form for the reflection positivity.\n\nThe reflection positivity needs a kernel on (U⁺) × (W⁺), i.e., (g₁, g₂) × (W₃, W₄). But the character expansion gives a kernel on (g₁, g₂, W₃) × (W₄), which is a different grouping.\n\nSo the character expansion does NOT give a Mercer-PD kernel on (U⁺) × (W⁺). This is the fundamental obstruction.\n\nOK, so I'm now fairly convinced that the character expansion / Mercer-PD approach does NOT work for the reflection positivity. The §8.11.59 design doc's claim of \"PD for each u⁰\" is WRONG.\n\nBut the §8.11.59 decision says the axiom IS true (by Osterwalder-Seiler). So the correct proof must use a DIFFERENT mechanism.\n\nLet me think about what that mechanism could be. The Osterwalder-Seiler proof (as I understand it from the literature) uses the following approach:\n\n1. The Boltzmann factor is a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors.\n2. Each plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD on its link variables (as a group).\n3. The product of PD functions is PD on the product group.\n4. The reflection positivity follows from the PD of the Boltzmann factor.\n\nStep 4 is the key. The Osterwalder-Seiler proof uses the fact that the reflection `θ` is an involution, and the reflection positivity integral can be written as a GROUP PD integral by a suitable change of variables.\n\nSpecifically, the reflection positivity integral is:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nThe key insight is that `exp(-β S(U))` is PD on the full link variable group. The PD means:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral is a SINGLE integral. But we can write it as:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nThe key: define `G(U) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2)`. Then:\n```\nG(U) · conj(G(θU)) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2) · conj(F((θU)⁺)) · exp(-β S_pos((θU)⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_int(θU)/2)\n```\n\nUsing `S_pos((θU)⁺) = S_pos(θ(U⁻)) = S_neg(U⁻)` (reflection symmetry) and `S_int(θU) = S_int(U)`:\n```\n= F(U⁺) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2)\n= F(U⁺) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U))\n```\n\nAnd `exp(-β S(U)) = exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)) · exp(-β S_int(U))`.\n\nSo `G(U) · conj(G(θU)) = F(U⁺) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)/2) · exp(-β S_int(U))`.\n\nAnd `I = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θ(U⁻))) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U) = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θ(U⁻))) exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)) exp(-β S_int(U)) dμ(U)`.\n\nSo `G(U) · conj(G(θU)) = I_integrand · exp(β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(β S_neg(U⁻)/2)`.\n\nHmm, that's `G(U) · conj(G(θU)) = I_integrand / (exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)/2))`. So `I_integrand = G(U) · conj(G(θU)) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)/2) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)/2)`.\n\nThis doesn't simplify to `G(U) · conj(G(θU))`.\n\nLet me try a different factorization. Define `G(U) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2)`. Then:\n```\nG(U) · conj(G(θU)) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S_pos(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S_int(θU)/2)\n= F(U⁺) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)) · exp(-β S_int(U))\n= F(U⁺) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S(U))\n= I_integrand\n```\n\nSo `I = ∫ G(U) · conj(G(θU)) dμ(U)`.\n\nNow, the group PD of `exp(-β S)` means:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nBut `I = ∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) dμ(U)` is NOT the same as the group PD integral. The group PD integral has `exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V))`, while `I` has `exp(-β S(U))` (with `V = θU`).\n\nSo `I = ∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)`, and the group PD has `exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V))`.\n\nIf `S(U) = S(U⁻¹ θU)`, then `I` would be a special case of the group PD (with `V = θU`). But `S(U) ≠ S(U⁻¹ θU)` in general.\n\nSo the group PD of `exp(-β S)` does NOT directly give the reflection positivity.\n\nHmm. I'm really stuck on the mathematics. Let me try a completely different approach: let me look at the Osterwalder-Seiler paper directly.\n\nActually, let me search for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof online.","turn":66,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":86,"role":"Tool","content":"Search results (5):\n\n1. [https://ai.vixra.org/pdf/2607.0073v1.pdf]\n   PDF A Machine-Checked Reflection-Positivity Framework for ZN Lattice Gauge ...\n   Abstract We machine-check, in Lean 4 with no sorry and no project axioms, the Osterwalder- Seiler reflection positivity of a lattice gauge theory with finite abelian gauge group. The development is organised so that the three ingredients are separated and each is proved on its own: an analytic step, a geometric step, and the single place where a property of the Boltzmann factor is actually used.\n\n2. [https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-113/issue-3/General-proof-of-Osterwalder-Schrader-positivity-for-the-Wilson-action/cmp/1104160284.pdf]\n   PDF General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity for the Wilson Action\n   Abstract We extend the proof by Osterwalder and Seiler of reflection positivity of lattice gauge theories with fermions to the case in which the reflection is performed with respect to planes containing sites. Our proof applies to all observables and relies on the particular structure of the Wilson action. This assures the positivity of the correlation functions\n\n3. [https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01221251]\n   General proof of Osterwalder-Schrader positivity for the ... - Springer\n   Abstract We extend the proof by Osterwalder and Seiler of reflection positivity of lattice gauge theories with fermions to the case in which the reflection is performed with respect to planes containing sites. Our proof applies to all observables and relies on the particular structure of the Wilson action.\n\n4. [https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Gauge-field-theories-on-a-lattice-Osterwalder-Seiler/610728f713f24d54488c5e5be85d5d96092211e3]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - Semantic Scholar\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n5. [https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0003491678900398]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - ScienceDirect\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n\nTime: 2026-08-10 01:40:48 UTC","timestamp":1786326048,"token_count":657,"tool_call_id":"e1e262d4-fab6-4e4a-b4e8-da45c3fce0bb","full_token_estimate":560,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"web_search","file_path":null,"old_text":"Osterwalder Seiler lattice gauge theory reflection positivity proof mechanism plaquette","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":2616,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1109,"edit_line":null},"turn":66,"is_prune_marker":false}
