{"id":89,"role":"Assistant","content":"I've done extensive analysis. Let me document my key findings. The mathematical analysis reveals important information about the proof strategy. Let me update my todo list and write the analysis to the design doc.\nTime: 2026-08-10 01:45:46 UTC","timestamp":1786326346,"token_count":57,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"task_items\":[{\"content\":\"Read §8.11.59 in design doc to understand the proof strategy\",\"id\":\"1\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"Study transferMatrix_change_of_variables (TransferMatrix.lean:2619) as the KEY PATTERN\",\"id\":\"2\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"Read transferMatrixPositivity_axiom (ReflectionPositivity.lean:2641) and its callers\",\"id\":\"3\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"Read key infrastructure: reflectLinkVariable_measurePreserving_between, neg_action_reflection_os_periodic, interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic\",\"id\":\"4\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"Read gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive and total_decomposition_os_periodic\",\"id\":\"5\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"Read plaquetteBoltzmannPD, interface_kernel_character_expansion, PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg\",\"id\":\"6\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"CRITICAL ANALYSIS: The existing infrastructure ALREADY performs the change of variables (step A done). The PD step (B) is the real challenge.\",\"id\":\"7\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"completed\"},{\"content\":\"Document findings in §8.11.60: 'PD for each u⁰' claim is WRONG; identify correct approach\",\"id\":\"8\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"in_progress\"}],\"action\":\"update\"}","name":"todo_list"},"id":"a37e7348-9118-4acf-975b-11c94c142888","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":6027,"reasoning_content":"The PDF is not readable in text form. Let me search for a more accessible version of the Osterwalder-Seiler proof mechanism. But actually, I've spent a lot of time on the mathematical analysis. Let me take stock of where I am and what I should do.\n\nKey findings from this session:\n1. The existing infrastructure (`measure_factorization'` + the lemma at TransferMatrix.lean:5160 + `transferMatrix_change_of_variables`) ALREADY performs the change of variables. So step (A) is already done.\n\n2. The PD step (B) is the hard part. The design doc's claim of \"PD for each u⁰\" is WRONG — the character expansion gives `Ψ_w(u⁰)` which is not non-negative (confirmed by §8.11.58 U(1) counterexample).\n\n3. The `PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg` lemma exists and gives `∫∫ f(x) conj(f(y)) K(x,y) dμ dμ ≥ 0` for Mercer-PD kernels. But the interface kernel is NOT Mercer-PD for each fixed u⁰.\n\n4. The §8.11.58 analysis shows that `∫ χ_s · |f|²` can be negative, so the character expansion approach doesn't directly give non-negativity.\n\nGiven my context is at 78%, I should document my findings and prepare for handoff. Let me:\n1. Document the analysis in the design doc (§8.11.60)\n2. Update the project task list\n3. Prepare a handoff\n\nActually, let me first check the build status to make sure the codebase is still green, then document my findings.\n\nLet me be efficient. Let me write the analysis to the design doc and update the task list, then handoff.\n\nActually, let me think about what concrete progress I can make. The key insight is that the existing infrastructure already does the change of variables. The remaining work is the PD step, which is mathematically subtle.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a different approach to the PD step that I haven't considered.\n\nActually, I just realized something. The §8.11.58 analysis shows that `∫ χ_s · |f|²` can be negative for a SINGLE character `χ_s`. But the full sum is `∑_w F(w) · ∫ Ψ_w · |A_w|²`. The question is whether the FULL SUM is non-negative, not whether each term is.\n\nThe §8.11.58 U(1) counterexample shows that a single term can be negative. But the full sum includes ALL terms, and the bulk Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S_pos)` (which is included in `A_w`) has non-negative Fourier coefficients. The §8.11.58 analysis notes this (point 1 in \"What remains open\"): \"Does the bulk Boltzmann factor change the picture?\"\n\nSo the key open question is: does the full sum `∑_w F(w) · ∫ Ψ_w · |A_w|²` (including the bulk Boltzmann factor) give non-negativity, even though individual terms can be negative?\n\nThis is a deep mathematical question that I can't resolve in this session. Let me document my findings and prepare for handoff.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The bulk Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` is PD on the positive-half link variables (as a group). This means it has a character expansion with non-negative coefficients. When convolved with `f`, the result `A_w = ∫ f · exp(-β S_pos) · Φ_w dμ⁺` includes the bulk Boltzmann factor.\n\nThe key insight might be that the bulk Boltzmann factor, being PD, has a specific structure that makes the full sum non-negative. But this is exactly the question that §8.11.58 says \"requires further investigation.\"\n\nLet me think about whether the Osterwalder-Seiler proof actually uses the bulk Boltzmann factor in a crucial way. In the Osterwalder-Seiler proof, the reflection positivity is:\n```\n∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ ≥ 0\n```\n\nThe Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S(U)) = exp(-β S_pos) · exp(-β S_neg) · exp(-β S_int)` includes the bulk factors. After the change of variables, the bulk factors become `exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_pos(W⁺))`, which are PD on U⁺ and W⁺ respectively.\n\nThe key might be that the bulk factors, being PD, can be absorbed into the function `H` in a way that makes the interface kernel PD. Specifically, if `exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` is PD on U⁺, then `H(U⁺) = f(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` has a specific structure (it's `f` times a PD function), and the interface kernel applied to this `H` might give non-negativity.\n\nBut this is still the same question: does the full sum give non-negativity?\n\nOK, I think the correct approach might be to NOT use the character expansion at all, but instead use the GROUP PD of the full Boltzmann factor directly. The group PD of `exp(-β S)` on the full link variable group gives:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nAnd the reflection positivity integral `I = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)` might be a SPECIAL CASE of this group PD integral, with a suitable choice of `G` and a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe key: if we set `V = θU` in the group PD integral, we get:\n```\n∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ θU)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nBut `S(U⁻¹ θU) ≠ S(U)` in general. So this doesn't directly give the reflection positivity.\n\nHOWEVER, if the action `S` has the property that `S(U⁻¹ θU) = S(U)` (or some related identity), then the reflection positivity would follow from the group PD. But this property doesn't hold in general.\n\nOK, I think I need to accept that I can't resolve the mathematical question in this session. Let me document my findings and prepare for handoff.\n\nLet me write a new section §8.11.60 documenting:\n1. The existing infrastructure already performs the change of variables (step A is done).\n2. The PD step (B) is the remaining challenge.\n3. The design doc's \"PD for each u⁰\" claim is WRONG (character expansion gives `Ψ_w(u⁰)` which is not non-negative).\n4. The `PositiveDefiniteKernel.integralOperator_nonneg` lemma exists but can't be applied directly.\n5. The key open question: does the full sum (including bulk Boltzmann factor) give non-negativity?\n6. Possible alternative: use group PD of the full Boltzmann factor directly.\n\nLet me write this to the design doc and update the task list.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe I'm wrong about the \"PD for each u⁰\" claim. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nThe interface kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, W⁺) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺))` is a product of plaquette Boltzmann factors. Each plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD on its 4 link variables (as a group). The product of PD functions on overlapping variables is PD on the product group.\n\nNow, \"PD on the product group\" means the kernel `K(x, y) = K(x⁻¹ y)` is PD, where `x` and `y` are full configs. But our kernel is `K(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺)`, not `K(x⁻¹ y)`.\n\nThe key question is: can we write `K(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺) = K'(x⁻¹ y)` for some PD function `K'` and some identification of `x` and `y`?\n\nIf we set `x = (U⁺, u⁰, 1)` and `y = (1, u⁰, θW⁺)`, then `x⁻¹ y = (U⁺⁻¹, 1, θW⁺)`. And `K'(x⁻¹ y) = K'(U⁺⁻¹, 1, θW⁺)`. But we want `K(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺)`, not `K'(U⁺⁻¹, 1, θW⁺)`.\n\nThe issue is that `K` depends on `U⁺` (not `U⁺⁻¹`) and `u⁰` (not `1`). So `K(U⁺, u⁰, θW⁺) ≠ K'(x⁻¹ y)` for any simple identification.\n\nBUT, the plaquette Boltzmann factor `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` IS of the form `φ(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹)` where `φ(x) = exp(c · Re Tr(x))` is PD on SU(N). Wait, no — `g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹` is a product of 4 matrices, not a single group element. And `φ` is PD on SU(N), not on SU(N)⁴.\n\nActually, the plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD on SU(N)⁴ (as a group), which means `B_p(g⁻¹ h) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁⁻¹h₁ g₂⁻¹h₂ (g₃⁻¹h₃)⁻¹ (g₄⁻¹h₄)⁻¹))` is PD. This is NOT the same as `φ(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹)` being PD on SU(N).\n\nSo the plaquette Boltzmann factor is PD on SU(N)⁴ (as a group), but the reflection positivity involves `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄)` (not `B_p(g⁻¹ h)`). The connection between the two is non-trivial.\n\nOK, I think the correct approach is to use the CHARACTER EXPANSION of the plaquette Boltzmann factor, but in a way that accounts for the reflection. The key insight (which I think I was missing) is that the character expansion of `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃⁻¹, g₄⁻¹)` gives:\n```\nB_p = ∑ coeff · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · conj(χ_u(g₃)) · conj(χ_v(g₄))\n```\n\nAfter the change of variables (g₃ = W₃⁻¹ temporal, g₄ = W₄ spatial):\n```\nB_p = ∑ coeff · χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · χ_u(W₃) · conj(χ_v(W₄))\n```\n\nThe W₃ factor is NOT conjugated (because the reflection inversion cancels the plaquette inversion for temporal links), and the W₄ factor IS conjugated (because the spatial reflection doesn't cancel the plaquette inversion).\n\nFor the Mercer-PD, we need ALL W⁺ factors to be conjugated. But W₃ is NOT conjugated. So the kernel is NOT Mercer-PD on (U⁺) × (W⁺).\n\nHOWEVER, if we group the variables differently — specifically, if we put the temporal W⁺ links with the U⁺ links and the spatial W⁺ links with the conjugated factors — then the kernel becomes:\n```\nK = ∑ coeff · [χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂) · χ_u(W₃)] · conj(χ_v(W₄))\n```\n\nThis is a kernel on (g₁, g₂, W₃) × (W₄), which is a DIFFERENT grouping than (U⁺) × (W⁺). The reflection positivity needs a kernel on (U⁺) × (W⁺), not on (g₁, g₂, W₃) × (W₄).\n\nSo the character expansion does NOT give a Mercer-PD kernel on (U⁺) × (W⁺). This is the fundamental obstruction.\n\nBUT, the Osterwalder-Seiler theorem DOES prove reflection positivity. So there must be a way around this. Let me think about what it is.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the reflection positivity is NOT about a kernel on (U⁺) × (W⁺). It's about a kernel on the FULL config space, with the reflection as the \"pairing\" between the two sides.\n\nThe group PD of the Boltzmann factor `exp(-β S)` on the full link variable group gives:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThe reflection positivity is:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nThe connection: if we define `G(U) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2)` (as I computed earlier), then `G(U) · conj(G(θU)) = F(U⁺) · conj(F(θ(U⁻))) · exp(-β S(U))`. So `I = ∫ G(U) · conj(G(θU)) dμ(U)`.\n\nNow, the group PD integral is `∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0`. If we set `V = θU`, we get `∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ θU)) dμ(U)`. But `S(U⁻¹ θU) ≠ S(U)` in general.\n\nSo the reflection positivity `I = ∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) dμ(U)` is NOT a special case of the group PD integral (which has `exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V))`, not `exp(-β S(U))`).\n\nThe difference is: the group PD has `exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V))` (the Boltzmann factor at the group product `U⁻¹ V`), while the reflection positivity has `exp(-β S(U))` (the Boltzmann factor at `U`, with `V = θU` absorbed into the function `G`).\n\nSo the group PD of the Boltzmann factor does NOT directly give the reflection positivity. The connection is more subtle.\n\nI think the Osterwalder-Seiler proof uses a DIFFERENT approach. Let me think about what it could be.\n\nActually, I think the Osterwalder-Seiler proof uses the fact that the Boltzmann factor can be decomposed as a product of \"half-Boltzmann factors\" that are PD as kernels. Specifically, each plaquette Boltzmann factor `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` can be written as a PD kernel on (g₁, g₂) × (g₃, g₄):\n```\nB_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = ∑_w F_w · Φ_w(g₁, g₂) · conj(Φ_w(g₃, g₄))\n```\n\nwhere `Φ_w(g₁, g₂) = χ_s(g₁) · χ_t(g₂)` and `F_w ≥ 0`. This is the character expansion, and it IS a Mercer-PD kernel on (g₁, g₂) × (g₃, g₄) (because all the \"right\" factors are conjugated).\n\nWait, but after the change of variables, the \"right\" factors are (W₃, W₄), and the character expansion gives `χ_u(W₃) · conj(χ_v(W₄))` — the W₃ factor is NOT conjugated. So it's NOT a Mercer-PD kernel on (g₁, g₂) × (W₃, W₄).\n\nThe issue is that the change of variables inverts the temporal links (W₃ = g₃⁻¹), which changes the character from `conj(χ_u(g₃))` to `χ_u(W₃)` (not conjugated). This breaks the Mercer-PD structure.\n\nSo the change of variables is the problem. Without the change of variables, the kernel IS Mercer-PD on (g₁, g₂) × (g₃, g₄). But with the change of variables, it's NOT.\n\nBUT, the change of variables is necessary to make the SAME function `H` appear on both sides (resolving the S_pos/S_neg asymmetry). Without the change of variables, the two sides have different functions (H on the U⁺ side and H' on the U⁻ side, where H' involves S_neg instead of S_pos).\n\nSo there's a tension: the change of variables is needed to make the same function appear on both sides, but it breaks the Mercer-PD structure.\n\nI think the resolution is that the change of variables should be applied to the FUNCTION, not the kernel. Specifically:\n- Keep the kernel as `B_p(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))` (which IS Mercer-PD on (g₁, g₂) × (g₃, g₄)).\n- Apply the change of variables to the function `F(θU⁺) = F(θ(U⁻))`, transforming it to a function of W⁺ = θ(U⁻).\n- The function `F(W⁺)` is NOT the same as `F(U⁺)` (it's a different function), but the kernel is Mercer-PD.\n\nWait, but then the two sides have different functions (F(U⁺) on the left and F(W⁺) on the right), and the Mercer-PD gives `∫∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(W⁺)) K(U⁺, W⁺) dμ dμ ≥ 0` only if F is the SAME function on both sides. If F(U⁺) ≠ F(W⁺) (as functions), then the Mercer-PD doesn't apply.\n\nHmm, but the Mercer-PD gives `∫∫ G(x) conj(G(y)) K(x, y) dμ dμ ≥ 0` for ANY function G. So if we set G = F (the same function on both sides), we get `∫∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(W⁺)) K(U⁺, W⁺) dμ dμ ≥ 0`. But in our integral, the left side is `F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` and the right side is `F(W⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(W⁺))`. These are the SAME function `H(X) = F(X) · exp(-β S_pos(X))` evaluated at U⁺ and W⁺. So the Mercer-PD gives `∫∫ H(U⁺) conj(H(W⁺)) K(U⁺, W⁺) dμ dμ ≥ 0` IF K is Mercer-PD.\n\nBut K is NOT Mercer-PD (because the change of variables breaks the structure). So this doesn't work.\n\nOK, I think the fundamental issue is that the change of variables and the Mercer-PD are INCOMPATIBLE. The change of variables is needed to make the same function appear on both sides, but it breaks the Mercer-PD structure of the kernel.\n\nThe resolution must be to NOT do the change of variables, and instead find a different way to handle the S_pos/S_neg asymmetry. OR, to find a different PD structure that is compatible with the change of variables.\n\nLet me think about the first option: NOT doing the change of variables. Without the change of variables, the integral is:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θ(U⁻))) exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) exp(-β S_neg(U⁻)) exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻)) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nThe kernel is `K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻))`, which IS Mercer-PD on (U⁺, u⁰) × (U⁻, u⁰) (because the character expansion gives all U⁻ factors conjugated).\n\nBut the two sides have DIFFERENT functions: `H₁(U⁺, u⁰) = F(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺))` on the left and `H₂(U⁻, u⁰) = F(θ(U⁻), u⁰_s) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻))` on the right. These are NOT the same function.\n\nThe Mercer-PD gives `∫∫ G(x) conj(G(y)) K(x, y) dμ dμ ≥ 0` for the SAME function G. If H₁ ≠ H₂, we can't directly apply it.\n\nBUT, we can write:\n```\nI = ∫∫ H₁(U⁺, u⁰) conj(H₂(U⁻, u⁰)) K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nIf K is Mercer-PD on (U⁺, u⁰) × (U⁻, u⁰), then:\n```\n∫∫ G(U⁺, u⁰) conj(G(U⁻, u⁰)) K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(u⁰) ≥ 0\n```\n\nfor any G. But we need G = H₁ on the left and G = H₂ on the right, and H₁ ≠ H₂. So we can't directly apply the Mercer-PD.\n\nHOWEVER, if we can write H₁ = H₂ (i.e., if `F(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) = F(θ(U⁻), u⁰_s) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻))` as functions), then we could apply the Mercer-PD. But this requires `F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) = F(θ(U⁻)) · exp(-β S_neg(U⁻))`, which is NOT true in general (U⁺ and U⁻ are independent variables).\n\nSo neither approach works directly. The change of variables breaks the Mercer-PD, and not doing the change of variables gives different functions on the two sides.\n\nI think the correct approach must be more subtle. Let me think about what the Osterwalder-Seiler proof actually does.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the Mercer-PD of the kernel `K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) = exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻))` on (U⁺, u⁰) × (U⁻, u⁰) gives:\n```\n∫∫∫ G(U⁺, u⁰) conj(G(U⁻, u⁰)) K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(u⁰) ≥ 0\n```\n\nfor any G. Note that this is a TRIPLE integral (over U⁺, U⁻, u⁰), with the SAME u⁰ on both sides.\n\nNow, our integral (without the change of variables) is:\n```\nI = ∫∫∫ H₁(U⁺, u⁰) conj(H₂(U⁻, u⁰)) K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nwhere H₁ ≠ H₂. The Mercer-PD gives non-negativity only when H₁ = H₂.\n\nBut wait — the Mercer-PD gives non-negativity for ANY G, not just when the two sides are the same. Actually, no — the Mercer-PD gives `∫∫ G(x) conj(G(y)) K(x, y) dμ(x) dμ(y) ≥ 0` for any G. The two sides are the SAME function G. If the two sides are different functions (H₁ ≠ H₂), the Mercer-PD does NOT give non-negativity.\n\nSo the key question is: can we write `I = ∫∫ G(x) conj(G(y)) K(x, y) dμ(x) dμ(y)` for some G?\n\nWithout the change of variables, H₁ ≠ H₂, so we can't. With the change of variables, H₁ = H₂ = H, but the kernel is NOT Mercer-PD.\n\nSo neither approach works directly. The correct approach must be something else.\n\nLet me think about this from the perspective of the POSITIVE OPERATOR. The transfer matrix T is a positive operator if `∫ g · Tg ≥ 0` for all g. The transfer matrix is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻)/2 + S_int(U))) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n```\n\nAfter the change of variables:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = exp(-β S⁺(u)/2) · ∫ ψ(merge(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β(S⁺(V⁺')/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θV⁺))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nThe transfer matrix positivity is `∫ g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ(u) ≥ 0`.\n\nThe transfer matrix can be written as `T = B* · B` if the kernel `exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θV⁺))` has a \"square root\" decomposition. The character expansion gives:\n```\nexp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, θV⁺)) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺))\n```\n\nIf `Ψ_w(u⁰) ≥ 0` for all w, then the kernel is a positive-coefficient sum of rank-1 terms, and T = B* · B. But `Ψ_w(u⁰)` is NOT non-negative (it's a product of characters).\n\nSo the `T = B* · B` decomposition doesn't work directly.\n\nI think the correct approach might be to use the GROUP PD of the interface kernel (not the Mercer PD). The interface kernel `exp(-β S_int(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻))` is PD on the interface link variable group (as a group, i.e., `K(x⁻¹ y)` is PD). The group PD gives:\n```\n∫∫ G(x) conj(G(y)) K(x⁻¹ y) dμ(x) dμ(y) ≥ 0\n```\n\nBut our integral has `K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻)`, not `K(x⁻¹ y)`. The connection is through the reflection: `K(U⁺, u⁰, U⁻) = K'(x⁻¹ y)` for some PD function K' and some identification of x and y.\n\nActually, I think the key is that the interface action `S_int` is a function of the plaquette variables, and each plaquette variable is a product of 4 link variables. The group PD of the plaquette Boltzmann factor on SU(N)⁴ gives:\n```\n∫∫ F(g) conj(F(h)) B_p(g⁻¹ h) dμ(g) dμ(h) ≥ 0\n```\n\nwhere `g⁻¹ h = (g₁⁻¹h₁, g₂⁻¹h₂, g₃⁻¹h₃, g₄⁻¹h₄)` and `B_p(g⁻¹ h) = exp(c · Re Tr(g₁⁻¹h₁ g₂⁻¹h₂ (g₃⁻¹h₃)⁻¹ (g₄⁻¹h₄)⁻¹))`.\n\nNow, if we set `h₁ = g₁ · a`, `h₂ = g₂ · b`, `h₃ = g₃ · c`, `h₄ = g₄ · d`, then `g⁻¹ h = (a, b, c⁻¹, d⁻¹)` and `B_p(g⁻¹ h) = exp(c · Re Tr(a b c d))`. Wait, that's `exp(c · Re Tr(a · b · (c⁻¹)⁻¹ · (d⁻¹)⁻¹)) = exp(c · Re Tr(a b c d))`. Hmm, that's not right.\n\nLet me redo: `B_p(g⁻¹ h) = exp(c · Re Tr((g₁⁻¹h₁)(g₂⁻¹h₂)(g₃⁻¹h₃)⁻¹(g₄⁻¹h₄)⁻¹))`. With `h₁ = g₁ · a`, `h₂ = g₂ · b`, `h₃ = g₃ · c`, `h₄ = g₄ · d`:\n- `g₁⁻¹h₁ = a`\n- `g₂⁻¹h₂ = b`\n- `g₃⁻¹h₃ = c`\n- `g₄⁻¹h₄ = d`\n- `(g₃⁻¹h₃)⁻¹ = c⁻¹`\n- `(g₄⁻¹h₄)⁻¹ = d⁻¹`\n\nSo `B_p(g⁻¹ h) = exp(c · Re Tr(a · b · c⁻¹ · d⁻¹)) = B_p(a, b, c, d)`.\n\nSo the group PD `∫∫ F(g) conj(F(h)) B_p(g⁻¹ h) dμ(g) dμ(h) ≥ 0` becomes `∫∫ F(g) conj(F(h)) B_p(a, b, c, d) dμ(g) dμ(h) ≥ 0` where `a = g₁⁻¹h₁`, etc.\n\nBut `a, b, c, d` depend on both `g` and `h`. So this is NOT the same as `∫∫ F̃(a, b) conj(F̃(c, d)) B_p(a, b, c, d) dμ(a) dμ(b) dμ(c) dμ(d) ≥ 0`.\n\nThe group PD integral is a double integral (over g and h), while the reflection positivity is a quadruple integral (over a, b, c, d). These are different.\n\nOK, I think I've exhausted my analysis. The key finding is that the connection between the group PD and the reflection positivity is non-trivial, and the character expansion / Mercer-PD approach doesn't directly work.\n\nLet me document my findings and prepare for handoff. I'll write a new section §8.11.60 to the design doc.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time. Maybe the correct approach is to use the group PD of the FULL Boltzmann factor (not just the interface kernel), and to NOT do the change of variables.\n\nThe group PD of `exp(-β S)` on the full link variable group gives:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThe reflection positivity is:\n```\nI = ∫ F(U⁺) conj(F(θU⁺)) exp(-β S(U)) dμ(U)\n```\n\nI showed that `I = ∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) dμ(U)` where `G(U) = F(U⁺) · exp(-β S_pos(U⁺)) · exp(-β S_int(U)/2)`.\n\nNow, the group PD integral is `∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0`. If we set `V = θU`, we get `∫ G(U) conj(G(θU)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ θU)) dμ(U)`. But `S(U⁻¹ θU) ≠ S(U)` in general.\n\nHowever, if we DON'T set `V = θU`, but instead integrate over ALL V, we get:\n```\n∫∫ G(U) conj(G(V)) exp(-β S(U⁻¹ V)) dμ(U) dμ(V) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThis is a double integral, while `I` is a single integral. So the group PD gives a WEAKER statement (non-negativity of a double integral) than what we need (non-negativity of a single integral).\n\nBut wait — can we relate the single integral to the double integral? If we set `V = θU` in the double integral, we get a single integral, but with `exp(-β S(U⁻¹ θU))` instead of `exp(-β S(U))`. These are different.\n\nSo the group PD of the full Boltzmann factor does NOT directly give the reflection positivity. The connection is non-trivial.\n\nI think the Osterwalder-Seiler proof must use a more sophisticated argument that I'm not seeing. Let me accept this and document my findings.\n\nLet me write the §8.11.60 section and prepare for handoff. I'm at 78% context, so I should handoff soon.\n\nLet me be efficient. Let me write the analysis to the design doc, update the task list, and handoff.","turn":69,"is_prune_marker":false}
